# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 have "moved_in_potency ∧ mover_in_act"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 has "moved_in_potency ∧ mover_in_act" and "moved_by_another"; they differ when only "nothing can be in motion except it is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion" and "a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "nothing can be in motion except it is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion" and "a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act" are true and "whatever is in motion is put in motion by another" is false. Is S3, "Now whatever is in motion is put in motion by another, for nothing can be in motion except it is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion; whereas a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act.", true then?
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 have "reduction_needs_actual"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 has "□reduction_needs_actual"; they differ when only "nothing can be reduced from potentiality to actuality except by something in a state of actuality" is true, which the first allows
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S5 is read aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 with no modality; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "can" does not say which it means
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "nothing can be reduced from potentiality to actuality except by something in a state of actuality" is true and "necessarily, nothing can be reduced from potentiality to actuality except by something in a state of actuality" is false. Is S5, "But nothing can be reduced from potentiality to actuality, except by something in a state of actuality.", true then?
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/readings: S11 is read 2 ways: aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 have "moved_by_another → first_mover ∨ infinite_regress"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it; they differ when only "whatever is in motion is put in motion by another" is true, which the second allows
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/answered: S11: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "whatever is in motion is put in motion by another" is true and "the series of movers each put in motion by another goes on to infinity" and "there is a first mover, put in motion by no other" are false. Is S11, "If that by which it is put in motion be itself put in motion, then this also must needs be put in motion by another, and that by another again.", true then?
aquinas-first-way.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 has "moved_in_potency ∧ mover_in_act ∧ motion_is_reduction ∧ reduction_needs_actual → □(self_mover → act_potency_same_respect)" and "□(motion_exists → self_mover ∨ moved_by_another)" and "moved_by_another → other_movers"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 has "□(moved_in_potency ∧ mover_in_act ∧ motion_is_reduction ∧ reduction_needs_actual → (self_mover → act_potency_same_respect))" and "motion_exists → (¬self_mover → moved_by_another)" and "motion_exists → other_movers"; they differ when only "in the world some things are in motion", "nothing can be in motion except it is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion", "a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act", "motion is nothing else than the reduction of something from potentiality to actuality", "nothing can be reduced from potentiality to actuality except by something in a state of actuality", "a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect, i.e. moves itself", "necessarily, (if (nothing can be in motion except it is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion and a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act and motion is nothing else than the reduction of something from potentiality to actuality and nothing can be reduced from potentiality to actuality except by something in a state of actuality), then (if a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect, i.e. moves itself, then the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect))", "necessarily, (if a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect, i.e. moves itself, then the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect)" and "necessarily, (if in the world some things are in motion, then (either a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect, i.e. moves itself or whatever is in motion is put in motion by another))" are true, which the first allows; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 has "moved_in_potency ∧ mover_in_act ∧ motion_is_reduction ∧ reduction_needs_actual → □(self_mover → act_potency_same_respect)" and "¬self_mover → moved_by_another" and "moved_by_another → infinite_regress ∨ first_mover" and "motion_exists ∧ moved_by_another → other_movers"
aquinas-first-way.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (nothing can be in motion except it is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion and a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act and motion is nothing else than the reduction of something from potentiality to actuality and nothing can be reduced from potentiality to actuality except by something in a state of actuality), then (necessarily, (if a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect, i.e. moves itself, then the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect))"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 does not
aquinas-first-way.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (nothing can be in motion except it is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion and a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act and motion is nothing else than the reduction of something from potentiality to actuality and nothing can be reduced from potentiality to actuality except by something in a state of actuality), then (necessarily, (if a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect, i.e. moves itself, then the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect))"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
aquinas-first-way.logic.r2: argue/readings: U2: aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 takes for granted "necessarily, (if in the world some things are in motion, then (either a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect, i.e. moves itself or whatever is in motion is put in motion by another))"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 do not
aquinas-first-way.logic.r2: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-first-way.logic.r2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "necessarily, (if in the world some things are in motion, then (either a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect, i.e. moves itself or whatever is in motion is put in motion by another))"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
